{"g9-q2-m1":{"fileId":"1fYWEF1s4FpoBt26kxcuCzStIloDlShoz","sourceSha256":"a6a466de73125b53be57c8eba6222afff8cd493b42086795ab7062f01618ca69","blocks":{"d4f685c40a7fdd49c445":{"type":"paragraph","page":11,"text":"Prose is basically a literary piece presented in the pattern of natural,","bbox":[108.0199966430664,144.0579833984375,526.8120727539062,156.89498901367188]},"426011fe4ea3457d2123":{"type":"paragraph","page":11,"text":"straightforward, and ordinary language written in sentences that are arranged in paragraphs. It does not pay much attention to metrical or rhythmic structures but is most concerned about the accuracy of grammatical structure, punctuations, capitalization, and syntax.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,158.0880126953125,526.8580322265625,212.72500610351562]},"41b8d782a50df7ce6f2f":{"type":"paragraph","page":11,"text":"Poetry is a special kind of artistic writing. It is a way of blending art with","bbox":[108.0199966430664,221.68798828125,526.8189086914062,234.52499389648438]},"774589ed7f63e71235f7":{"type":"paragraph","page":11,"text":"language. Repetition and rhythm are often used as building blocks for poetry. Poems use well-chosen words which are so intricately woven to convey lots of ideas, making each word very essential.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,235.68798828125,526.966064453125,276.3249816894531]},"2999d78c083faa71ab6c":{"type":"table","bbox":[72.088159661544,285.62000274658203,523.5234057617188,597.1199951171875],"text":"Category  Prose Poetry Structure/Form  Written in sentences forming paragraphs Written in lines forming stanzas/verses Syntax  Observes strict grammar, punctuation, and capitalization Has loose grammar and punctuation Has poetic license Kinds/Types  Common examples are essays, novels, journals, articles, blogs, news, speeches, diary entries, etc. Common examples are: Lyric– elegy, ode, and sonnet Dramatic– tragedy, comedy, tragic-comedy, closed drama, and masque Narrative– epic, ballad, tale, metrical, and romance Language Style  Usually straightforward, ordinary, and natural No limit in terms of word usage Uses creative, concise, articulate words Uses figurative Language and is rhythmical Content/Purpose  Informational, logical, practical Emotional  Now that you have a clearer understanding on the difference between prose   and poetry, you are now ready to study the elements of poetry!   ","page":11,"rows":[["Category","","Prose","Poetry"],["Structure/Form","","Written in sentences forming paragraphs","Written in lines forming stanzas/verses"],["Syntax","","Observes strict grammar, punctuation, and capitalization","Has loose grammar and punctuation Has poetic license"],["Kinds/Types","","Common examples are essays, novels, journals, articles, blogs, news, speeches, diary entries, etc.","Common examples are: Lyric– elegy, ode, and sonnet Dramatic– tragedy, comedy, tragic-comedy, closed drama, and masque Narrative– epic, ballad, tale, metrical, and romance"],["Language Style","","Usually straightforward, ordinary, and natural No limit in terms of word usage","Uses creative, concise, articulate words Uses figurative Language and is rhythmical"],["Content/Purpose","","Informational, logical, practical","Emotional"],["","Now that you have a clearer understanding on the difference between prose","",""],["and poetry, you are now ready to study the elements of poetry!","","",""]]},"43d56169d7360f28aa0f":{"type":"table","bbox":[72.088159661544,610.125,523.5234057617188,766.9749755859375],"text":"   Elements of Poetry  The following are the most common elements of poetry:     1. 2. Stanza– a group of lines that form the basic metrical unit in a poem or verse.     Stanzas may be classified into different types, such as couplet- two lines that     rhyme, tercet-three lines that may or may not rhyme, quatrain-four lines,     cinquain-five lines, sestet-six lines septet-seven lines, and octave-eight lines.     Three-lined poems up to eight-lined poems may or may not rhyme.     Rhyme– the repetition of the same or similar sounds at the end of each line.     It is achieved when two or more words have the same sound when spoken or     read, though these words may not have the same spelling. This rhyme creates     a pattern within the poem, called rhyme scheme, which makes the poem more     interesting.   ","page":11,"rows":[["","","","Elements of Poetry",""],["The following are the most common elements of poetry:","","","",""],["1. 2.","Stanza– a group of lines that form the basic metrical unit in a poem or verse.","","",""],["","Stanzas may be classified into different types, such as couplet- two lines that","","",""],["","rhyme, tercet-three lines that may or may not rhyme, quatrain-four lines,","","",""],["","cinquain-five lines, sestet-six lines septet-seven lines, and octave-eight lines.","","",""],["","Three-lined poems up to eight-lined poems may or may not rhyme.","","",""],["","Rhyme– the repetition of the same or similar sounds at the end of each line.","","",""],["","It is achieved when two or more words have the same sound when spoken or","","",""],["","read, though these words may not have the same spelling. This rhyme creates","","",""],["","a pattern within the poem, called rhyme scheme, which makes the poem more","","",""],["","interesting.","","",""]]},"ec48448aeae07a77651a":{"type":"paragraph","page":12,"text":"3. Rhythm– the pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables in each line, giving the poem a regular beat. Each group of stressed and unstressed syllable is called “foot”. One kind of metered foot is the iamb, which is a unit of rhythm consisting of one unstressed (ˇ), followed by one stressed (/) syllable. Iambic pentameter consists of five iambs in a single line. 4. Tone– the attitude of the writer about a subject. This is created by word choice, setting, regular or irregular meter, and figurative language. Mood– the emotion evoked by the author. It is the feeling or the atmosphere that the reader gets when he is reading a text. 5. Imagery– the use of vivid words that appeal to the five senses in order to deepen the reader’s understanding of the text. It also means the use of figurative language to represent ideas in a way that appeals to our physical senses. It usually creates an image in the reader’s mind. 6. Figurative Language– refers to a language that conveys deeper and more complex meaning of words than what it literally suggests. It is achieved mostly when identifying or comparing one to another. The most common figures of speech used in poems are simile, metaphor, personification, hyperbole, irony, alliteration,","bbox":[90.0250015258789,72.0579833984375,526.89404296875,306.3450012207031]},"70c86cc2204de07461ec":{"type":"paragraph","page":12,"text":"assonance,","bbox":[184.5440216064453,293.50799560546875,247.80502319335938,306.3450012207031]},"25ba237de17e9ecfd9db":{"type":"paragraph","page":12,"text":"consonance,","bbox":[258.67303466796875,293.50799560546875,329.3150939941406,306.3450012207031]},"19f98e7cc7bd05d68471":{"type":"paragraph","page":12,"text":"onomatopoeia,","bbox":[340.18310546875,293.50799560546875,422.23211669921875,306.3450012207031]},"cdad76fc28b47cd90689":{"type":"paragraph","page":12,"text":"repetition,","bbox":[433.2981262207031,293.50799560546875,491.75213623046875,306.3450012207031]},"593e2f44dc6106ec6802":{"type":"paragraph","page":12,"text":"and","bbox":[502.8181457519531,293.50799560546875,526.5671997070312,306.3450012207031]},"9224b1e277c7249092d7":{"type":"paragraph","page":12,"text":"symbolism.","bbox":[108.0199966430664,306.50799560546875,172.77000427246094,319.3450012207031]},"dbceee3ad15b9aa4ab83":{"type":"heading","page":19,"text":"ADVERB","bbox":[273.2699890136719,144.0579833984375,325.8399963378906,156.87298583984375]},"d81536732996d994048c":{"type":"paragraph","page":19,"text":"An adverb is a part of speech that simply acts or functions to modify an","bbox":[108.0199966430664,158.88800048828125,498.5539855957031,171.72500610351562]},"c0dd94b5fef8eaa12587":{"type":"paragraph","page":19,"text":"adjective, a verb, or another adverb. It answers the questions: When? Where? How? To what extent? Simply, it tells us how a thing is done, and when and where it is done. Examples:","bbox":[72.0250015258789,173.68798828125,524.7791137695312,239.12503051757812]},"651ce99c7f28f5954272":{"type":"heading","page":19,"text":"1. An adverb describes an adjective.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,249.0880126953125,281.8399963378906,261.90301513671875]},"3fa02cb88567c619613e":{"type":"paragraph","page":19,"text":"The doctors are extremely busy attending COVID-19 patients.","bbox":[108.0199966430664,272.10797119140625,447.4700012207031,284.9449768066406]},"c7ada471bdff3f71a406":{"type":"paragraph","page":19,"text":"What adjective describes doctors? Busy. How busy? Extremely is the adverb.","bbox":[144.0500030517578,294.91802978515625,360.2200012207031,330.6100158691406]},"dfdfc96b93eab6d4d753":{"type":"heading","page":19,"text":"2. It describes another adverb.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,340.50799560546875,251.02000427246094,353.322998046875]},"3eb5b4796e4e2951cc22":{"type":"paragraph","page":19,"text":"The victim acted quite suspiciously.","bbox":[108.0199966430664,363.50799560546875,307.6199951171875,376.3450012207031]},"f5b8ff93b596db05c0e1":{"type":"paragraph","page":19,"text":"How did the victim act? = suspiciously (adverb) How suspiciously? = quite (adverb describing another adverb)","bbox":[144.0500030517578,386.3380126953125,472.8499755859375,422.0299987792969]},"f90cb5113e2ead09b93f":{"type":"heading","page":19,"text":"3. It describes a verb.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,431.93798828125,199.19000244140625,444.75299072265625]},"209629b81f03a6689bc3":{"type":"paragraph","page":19,"text":"The sun shines brightly amid the rain.","bbox":[108.0199966430664,454.93798828125,321.6199951171875,467.8299865722656]},"e3fd53d6cb42e614aa4e":{"type":"paragraph","page":19,"text":"What did the sun do? Shines (verb) How did it shine? = brightly (adverb)","bbox":[144.0500030517578,477.7380065917969,340,513.4500122070312]}},"excerptBlocks":{"concepts:0":["d4f685c40a7fdd49c445","426011fe4ea3457d2123","41b8d782a50df7ce6f2f","774589ed7f63e71235f7","2999d78c083faa71ab6c","43d56169d7360f28aa0f"],"concepts:1":["ec48448aeae07a77651a","70c86cc2204de07461ec","25ba237de17e9ecfd9db","19f98e7cc7bd05d68471","cdad76fc28b47cd90689","593e2f44dc6106ec6802","9224b1e277c7249092d7"],"concepts:2":["dbceee3ad15b9aa4ab83","d81536732996d994048c","c0dd94b5fef8eaa12587","651ce99c7f28f5954272","3fa02cb88567c619613e","c7ada471bdff3f71a406","dfdfc96b93eab6d4d753","3eb5b4796e4e2951cc22","f5b8ff93b596db05c0e1","f90cb5113e2ead09b93f","209629b81f03a6689bc3","e3fd53d6cb42e614aa4e"]},"assets":{},"sourceSections":[{"id":"concepts","title":"Concepts & Rules","kind":"discussion","entries":[{"page":11,"blockIds":["d4f685c40a7fdd49c445","426011fe4ea3457d2123","41b8d782a50df7ce6f2f","774589ed7f63e71235f7","2999d78c083faa71ab6c","43d56169d7360f28aa0f"]},{"page":12,"blockIds":["ec48448aeae07a77651a","70c86cc2204de07461ec","25ba237de17e9ecfd9db","19f98e7cc7bd05d68471","cdad76fc28b47cd90689","593e2f44dc6106ec6802","9224b1e277c7249092d7"]},{"page":19,"blockIds":["dbceee3ad15b9aa4ab83","d81536732996d994048c","c0dd94b5fef8eaa12587","651ce99c7f28f5954272","3fa02cb88567c619613e","c7ada471bdff3f71a406","dfdfc96b93eab6d4d753","3eb5b4796e4e2951cc22","f5b8ff93b596db05c0e1","f90cb5113e2ead09b93f","209629b81f03a6689bc3","e3fd53d6cb42e614aa4e"]}]}]},"g9-q2-m2":{"fileId":"1sE1_PlqARG59kCtim19js5G9FFvB9x1O","sourceSha256":"f3025baefca371cb635ee98a031fdf8cf2ddc3cc02918cad8afee16c7292949c","blocks":{"9de1ab104b863cf753f8":{"type":"paragraph","page":13,"text":"LITERARY AND RHETORICAL DEVICES","bbox":[184.64999389648438,144.0579833984375,414.25,156.89498901367188]},"9c0bbc9b24f5599fe65f":{"type":"paragraph","page":13,"text":"Martin Luther King Jr.’s speech, I Have a Dream,” used some rhetorical and","bbox":[108.0199966430664,158.0880126953125,519.7310791015625,170.98001098632812]},"d711aa5d840d5bd6dda9":{"type":"paragraph","page":13,"text":"literary devices.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,171.88800048828125,163.37001037597656,184.72500610351562]},"125bdcada63003a8ade2":{"type":"heading","page":13,"text":"What is a rhetorical device?","bbox":[72.0250015258789,185.88800048828125,240.62001037597656,198.7030029296875]},"af0018e2cc57f73d24e4":{"type":"paragraph","page":13,"text":"Rhetorical device is a technique that an author or speaker uses to convey to","bbox":[108.0199966430664,199.88800048828125,526.7259521484375,212.77999877929688]},"025435440c724fbcdf0a":{"type":"paragraph","page":13,"text":"the listener or reader a meaning with the goal of persuading them. Rhetorical devices evoke an emotional response in the readers through the use of language.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,213.68798828125,526.358154296875,254.52499389648438]},"cfcd0f40d33aeac22610":{"type":"heading","page":13,"text":"What is meant by literary devices?","bbox":[72.0250015258789,255.48797607421875,278.6199951171875,268.302978515625]},"3c5d0b70ee01212b7c4f":{"type":"paragraph","page":13,"text":"Literary device is a technique a writer uses to produce a special effect in his","bbox":[108.0199966430664,268.50799560546875,527.1000366210938,281.3450012207031]},"6d0f8dd5a6417495214b":{"type":"paragraph","page":13,"text":"writing and to help readers understand his writing on a deeper level.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,281.3079833984375,444.66998291015625,294.1449890136719]},"5e33e825fc4f15ddb1ef":{"type":"paragraph","page":13,"text":"Study the table below which contains the meanings and examples of rhetorical","bbox":[108.0199966430664,307.10797119140625,527.0160522460938,319.9449768066406]},"d10adf426a138476092d":{"type":"paragraph","page":13,"text":"and literary devices:","bbox":[72.0250015258789,320.1180114746094,183.99000549316406,332.95501708984375]},"52ee55e3f6d5eea54142":{"type":"table","rows":[["Rhetorical/Literary Device","Meaning","Examples"],["Metaphor","a figure of speech in which a word or phrase is applied to an object or action to which it is not literally applicable","1. Kisses are the flowers of affection. 2. The falling snowflakes are dancers."],["Analogy","a comparison between two things, typically for the purpose of explanation or clarification","The structure of an atom is like a solar system. The nucleus is the sun, and electrons are the planets revolving around their sun."],["Parallelism","used to persuade, motivate, and/or evoke emotional responses in an audience and is often used in speeches. The balance between clauses or phrases makes complex thoughts easier to process while holding the reader's or listener's attention","1. \"My fellow Americans, ask not what your country can do for you, ask what you can do for your country.\" - John F. Kennedy 2. \"I don't want to live on in my work. I want to live on in my apartment.\" - Woody Allen"],["Repetition","is the repeating of a word or phrase. It is a common rhetorical device used to add emphasis and stress in writing and speech","1. “Let it snow, let it snow, let it snow.” 2. \"And miles to go before I sleep, and miles to go before I sleep.\" - Robert Frost \"Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening"]],"bbox":[72.19166882832845,333.23000717163086,523.403330485026,748.6250152587891],"text":"Rhetorical/Literary Device Meaning Examples Metaphor a figure of speech in which a word or phrase is applied to an object or action to which it is not literally applicable 1. Kisses are the flowers of affection. 2. The falling snowflakes are dancers. Analogy a comparison between two things, typically for the purpose of explanation or clarification The structure of an atom is like a solar system. The nucleus is the sun, and electrons are the planets revolving around their sun. Parallelism used to persuade, motivate, and/or evoke emotional responses in an audience and is often used in speeches. The balance between clauses or phrases makes complex thoughts easier to process while holding the reader's or listener's attention 1. \"My fellow Americans, ask not what your country can do for you, ask what you can do for your country.\" - John F. Kennedy 2. \"I don't want to live on in my work. I want to live on in my apartment.\" - Woody Allen Repetition is the repeating of a word or phrase. It is a common rhetorical device used to add emphasis and stress in writing and speech 1. “Let it snow, let it snow, let it snow.” 2. \"And miles to go before I sleep, and miles to go before I sleep.\" - Robert Frost \"Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening","page":13},"1004e874444fb451e680":{"type":"heading","page":14,"text":"TONE AND MOOD","bbox":[72.0250015258789,72.0579833984375,176.99000549316406,84.87298583984375]},"8801ce6b135c0e0f7348":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"Tone and mood both deal with the emotions centered on a piece of writing.","bbox":[108.0199966430664,85.0579833984375,526.6489868164062,97.94998168945312]},"0c1d65dc68afe4537113":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"Though they seem similar and can in fact be related casually, they are in fact different.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,98.85797119140625,526.4459838867188,125.6949691772461]},"c5d5c703072a9cea62d6":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"Tone is the author’s attitude towards a subject. While journalistic writing","bbox":[108.0199966430664,126.85797119140625,526.596923828125,139.69497680664062]},"310d93897cb3e92baf08":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"theoretically has a tone of distance and objectivity, all other writings can have various tones.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,140.66802978515625,526.784912109375,167.52499389648438]},"1bbeb98370ed84d515a4":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"Some tone words include:","bbox":[108.0199966430664,168.68798828125,250.19000244140625,181.52499389648438]},"14a529b2f2f32b33d81d":{"type":"table","bbox":[72.15833536783855,182.59996032714844,523.4366658528646,274.29002685546874],"text":"Positive tone words  Neutral Negative tone words Admiring Affectionate Benevolent Calm Casual Interested Joyful Lively Nostalgic Passionate Commanding Direct Impartial Indirect Meditative Annoyed Anxious Apathetic Bewildered Bitter","page":14,"rows":[["Positive tone words","","Neutral","Negative tone words"],["Admiring Affectionate Benevolent Calm Casual","Interested Joyful Lively Nostalgic Passionate","Commanding Direct Impartial Indirect Meditative","Annoyed Anxious Apathetic Bewildered Bitter"]]},"344bb6c2f4f9e0e58d80":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"Mood is the atmosphere of a piece of writing; it’s the emotion that a selection","bbox":[108.0199966430664,288.3079833984375,527.01904296875,301.1449890136719]},"49eab64b397979ab273e":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"arouses in a reader.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,302.3079833984375,186.37001037597656,315.1449890136719]},"8a85bef997d0de5600e5":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"Some common mood descriptors are:","bbox":[108.0199966430664,316.3079833984375,311.0199890136719,329.1449890136719]},"cccf7287ed09a3147bdc":{"type":"table","bbox":[72.15833536783855,330.2133382161458,523.4366658528646,408.94000854492185],"text":"Positive mood words  Negative mood words  Amused Bouncy Calm Cheerful Confident Mellow Nostalgic Optimistic Passionate Peaceful Aggravated Dreary Embarrassed Exhausted Nauseated Numb Overwhelmed Painful Pessimistic Lethargic","page":14,"rows":[["Positive mood words","","Negative mood words",""],["Amused Bouncy Calm Cheerful Confident","Mellow Nostalgic Optimistic Passionate Peaceful","Aggravated Dreary Embarrassed Exhausted Nauseated","Numb Overwhelmed Painful Pessimistic Lethargic"]]},"c4111e5d62585d743abe":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"AUTHOR’S PURPOSE","bbox":[237.8800048828125,429.93798828125,361.25,442.7749938964844]},"79dc4d715c4650dac6bb":{"type":"heading","page":14,"text":"Three Reasons for Writing","bbox":[72.0250015258789,450.5379943847656,225.4199981689453,463.3529968261719]},"266ef12d861e525013b9":{"type":"heading","page":14,"text":"1. Writing to Entertain","bbox":[72.0250015258789,463.5379943847656,207.010009765625,476.3529968261719]},"2f7265e005fa50bee405":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"The primary purpose of texts that are written to entertain is to amuse readers.","bbox":[108.0199966430664,476.3380126953125,530.489990234375,489.1750183105469]},"3aa767bb8f92dec28d56":{"type":"heading","page":14,"text":"Examples of Texts that Are Written to Entertain:","bbox":[72.0250015258789,489.5580139160156,356.239990234375,502.3730163574219]},"1623bc069e513aba3782":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"Stories","bbox":[99.0250015258789,502.16802978515625,139.77000427246094,515.0050048828125]},"2f79ddac498330846301":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"Dramas","bbox":[342.70001220703125,502.16802978515625,388.6499938964844,515.0050048828125]},"bea6b64b3f825125adbb":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"▪","bbox":[81.0250015258789,502.7400207519531,88.40499877929688,514.02001953125]},"a6a05192e1ba60d3a5da":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"▪","bbox":[324.70001220703125,502.7400207519531,332.0799865722656,514.02001953125]},"63537b9a581bf5cab83e":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"▪ Poems","bbox":[81.0250015258789,515.72998046875,137.37001037597656,528.0050048828125]},"60ab9f05bf4973d2e39a":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"▪ Songs","bbox":[324.70001220703125,515.72998046875,378.6499938964844,528.0050048828125]},"d46530e04c61b8fad0cf":{"type":"heading","page":14,"text":"2. Writing to Inform","bbox":[72.0250015258789,541.1680297851562,190.99000549316406,553.9830322265625]},"7bbb64f5a15b66b0713a":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"The primary purpose of texts that are written to inform is to enlighten the","bbox":[108.0199966430664,553.7579956054688,526.5589599609375,566.594970703125]},"21e6e8b1c3ef21cf490e":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"reader or to provide the reader with information about a topic.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,566.7579956054688,410.4499816894531,579.594970703125]},"e53cb924ca005df7b70a":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"Examples of Texts That Are Written to Inform: ▪","bbox":[72.0250015258789,592.1580200195312,343.0199890136719,617.449951171875]},"b5f97239bd44143da6a8":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"Expository Essays or Articles ▪ Instructions or Directions Encyclopedias or Other Reference Texts ▪","bbox":[99.0250015258789,605.5880126953125,316.0199890136719,643.25]},"5ce97aea8e498a619f7e":{"type":"heading","page":14,"text":"3.Writing to Persuade","bbox":[72.0250015258789,658.3880004882812,199.79000854492188,671.2030029296875]},"29d053a0fec0ea566e93":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"The purpose of the texts written to persuade is to compel readers to take","bbox":[108.0199966430664,672.18798828125,526.4379272460938,685.0249633789062]},"6c4814f76b386b7f24f4":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"action, to convince them of an idea through argument, or to reaffirm their existing beliefs. Examples of Texts That Are Written to Persuade: ▪","bbox":[72.0250015258789,686.18798828125,526.5518798828125,738.6799926757812]},"285503268ebafe205be9":{"type":"paragraph","page":14,"text":"Advertisements ▪ Campaign Speeches ▪ Persuasive Letters or Notes","bbox":[99.0250015258789,726.8079833984375,248.40000915527344,765.449951171875]},"c35d0ff6329d45466a06":{"type":"heading","page":18,"text":"COMPARING AND CONTRASTING","bbox":[202.8800048828125,301.3079833984375,396.2599792480469,314.12298583984375]},"e2593f760676898dea63":{"type":"paragraph","page":18,"text":"Compare and contrast is a rhetorical style that discusses the similarities and","bbox":[108.0199966430664,314.10797119140625,527.1000366210938,326.9449768066406]},"7b862b297f396e03073a":{"type":"paragraph","page":18,"text":"differences of two or more things: ideas, concepts, items, places, etc. This is also used to make some kind of comparison to help illustrate a point.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,327.1180114746094,527.1000366210938,352.7449951171875]},"99d50d5d34f34df445ad":{"type":"paragraph","page":18,"text":"A Venn diagram is used in making comparison and contrast to show","bbox":[108.0199966430664,352.9079895019531,526.6781005859375,365.79998779296875]},"8bc164062d053a6d473a":{"type":"paragraph","page":18,"text":"differences and similarities of two subjects. It uses overlapping circles or other shapes to illustrate the logical relationship between two or more sets of items. Often, they serve to graphically organize things, highlighting how the items are similar and different.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,365.7080078125,526.9600219726562,417.375]},"7f3e13f3e721e4fa86f9":{"type":"paragraph","page":18,"text":"Study the illustration below.","bbox":[108.0199966430664,417.5379943847656,260.17999267578125,430.42999267578125]},"035a120daad99d20a5b1":{"type":"figure","page":18,"bbox":[132,433.1400146484375,471,634.8900146484375],"src":"/assets/curriculum/figures/ba32587bf26bf736f765cf23.webp","width":1280,"height":560,"monochrome":true},"b635e086175829ed0fe7":{"type":"paragraph","page":18,"text":"Comparing Your Two friends","bbox":[232.47000122070312,445.13800048828125,390.04998779296875,457.9750061035156]},"8b8ccff0214c66a2446c":{"type":"heading","page":18,"text":"Differences","bbox":[363.76800537109375,495.96002197265625,376.5830078125,564.3299560546875]},"8379d246e17116b48b8f":{"type":"heading","page":18,"text":"Differences","bbox":[215.3179931640625,496.9900207519531,228.13299560546875,565.3299560546875]},"fefc07416fa73fc21b44":{"type":"heading","page":18,"text":"Similarities","bbox":[272.0799865722656,529.1100463867188,335.8999938964844,540.760009765625]},"65805726efbb6fb9d955":{"type":"heading","page":18,"text":"DRAWING CONCLUSIONS AND INFERENCES","bbox":[171.4499969482422,654.5880126953125,427.8699951171875,667.4030151367188]},"aed1fea88508276c98c8":{"type":"diagram","bbox":[70.625,667.5499877929688,524.9749755859375,759.1749877929688],"width":454.35,"height":91.62,"shapes":[{"d":"M0.00,0.00 h454.35 v13.00 h-454.35 Z","stroke":"none","fill":"#ffffff"